  "slug": "rna-high-precision-98a1",
  "summary": "Population-scale resequencing, genomic prediction, and bias-corrected domestication inference are now tractable for breeding and evolutionary questions.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is coupling genome-scale variation data with downstream inference for breeding and evolutionary questions. The crimson clover study re-sequences 194 accessions to yield variant catalogs, population structure, and a public 28,913-SNP genotyping panel for breeding. The maize landrace study introduces GP-GO-Hs, combining genomic prediction with genomic offset and gene diversity across 397 landraces in 25 European environments. The walnut paper targets inference bias instead: a modified STRUCTURE parameterization reduces sampling and effective population size biases, supporting a South Asian origin. What is new is explicit correction of population-genomic models and their use for breeding and environment-specific prediction, though the clover work is more applied infrastructure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-30"
}
